  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing CSS Flipping Flaws**\n\nI\'m currently investigating the mirroring and bleed-through problems encountered with the CSS 3D flip effect on the card element. My focus is on pinpointing the root cause. My preliminary observations suggest there might be issues with how the transformations are being handled or, potentially, with the z-index stacking of elements involved in the flip.\n\n\n**Pinpointing 3D Rendering Flaws**\n\nI\'ve made a significant breakthrough: the flattening of the 3D rendering context, triggered by `overflow: hidden` or `mix-blend-mode` within a `transform-style: preserve-3d` container, is the culprit. This explains the observed mirroring and bleed-through. The front face elements (logo and text) are effectively visible mirrored on the back, and the overlap problem with stamps is also resolved as a consequence of the flattening.\n\n\n**Implementing Robust CSS Fixes**\n\nI\'ve formulated a deterministic CSS and React solution for the 3D flip effect\'s mirroring and bleed-through. The core is explicitly setting `transform` and `backface-visibility` on the front and back faces, and isolating elements to avoid breaking `preserve-3d`. To ensure zero bleed-through or mirroring across all browsers, I\'m isolating each face\'s content and toggling `opacity` and `pointer-events` on inactive faces.\n\n\n**Refining Flip Layout & UX**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the layout and user experience of the flip. I\'ve significantly reduced the size of the stamp icons and implemented a compact 2x5 grid layout to improve visual clarity and space utilization. I\'ve also incorporated the user\'s request, dynamically generating a scannable QR code using `react-qr-code`, ensuring it\'s standards-compliant and works offline within the PWA, replacing the placeholder image.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_681352"
